    {
      "slug": "captain-aardvark",
      "name": "Captain Aardvark",
      "type": "person",
      "description": "Captain \"Aarfy\" Aardvark is a fictional character, a major antagonist in the 1961 novel Catch-22 by Joseph Heller. Aarfy is the plump navigator in Yossarian's B-25, noted for being oblivious to incoming flak, getting lost on missions, and his omnipresent pipe. His nickname \"Aarfy\" is an abbreviation of his surname, Aardvark.\n\nAarfy is a social climber looking to work his way up into high society.\n\nAarfy befriends Nately because Nately's father is rich, and he hopes to work for him after the war.\n\nAarfy perceives himself as moral and protects well-connected women from the sexual advances of other officers, looking for any possibility of self-advance within society. \"Old Aarfy has never paid for it\", he often says in reference to prostitutes. He often and openly commits rape, and says he can tell which girl is \"nice and clean\" and which is not.",
      "short_description": "Captain \"Aarfy\" Aardvark is a fictional character, a major antagonist in the 1961 novel Catch-22 by Joseph Heller. Aarfy is the plump navigator in Yossarian's B-25, noted for being oblivious to incoming flak, getting…",
